{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to 80,000 Hours, a project of \nEffective Ventures, for supporting PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5800,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From hoverboards to flying cars to cloud cities, \nanti-gravity is a staple of science fiction and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11520,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our dream of a less Earth-bound future. But \nin the real universe gravity appears to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a purely attractive force. Feels like \nits main MO is keeping us stuck to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21640,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface of this lonely rock. But maybe if we \nscience hard enough we can remove the fiction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27520,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from science fiction. So for the sake of our flying \ncars we should at least try. And for many years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists have wondered whether a \ncertain well-known exotic material may  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38560,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experience gravitational repulsion from \nthe Earth. That material is antimatter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and physicists at CERN have just completed \na very long and very difficult experiment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50280,
    "dDurationMs": 9640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to answer a seemingly simple question: does \nantimatter fall down, or does it fall up?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62320,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "—"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Antimatter—the evil twin of regular matter. Or \nis it just the misunderstood twin of regular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter? We’ve made it in labs and seen that \nan antimatter particle has charge and other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75480,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum properties compared to its regular \nmatter counterpart. The only thing that’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81600,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same is its mass. Except it may be that in at \nleast one sense its mass may be different—even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opposite to regular matter—and that fact may \ngive us our antigravity. Or at least teach us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "profound truths about the nature of reality. \nThe trick is going to be to think about mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not as one property of an object, but rather as \ntwo distinct and independent properties. There’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inertial mass—the resistance to being shoved \nor slowed. And there’s gravitational mass,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which determines the heft of your gravitational \nfield, and your response to other such fields.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we’ve explored an aspect of this idea \npreviously, and this is a good review episode  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120920,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the studious, or a good followup episode for \nthe impatient. Today we’re going to focus on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127520,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anti-matter and the flying car implications \nof splitting inertial and gravitational mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133560,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with Isaac Newton’s description of \nall of this. We’ll move to Einstein’s upgrade  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137920,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "later. Inertial mass is the mass in Newton’s \nsecond law. It determines how much force you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to apply to achieve a give acceleration. On \nthe other hand gravitational mass is the mass in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newton’s law of universal gravitation. It acts as \nthe gravitational charge, determining the strength  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154600,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the gravitational field and the strength of \nresponse to another such field. We almost always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159760,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assume that inertial mass and gravitational \nmass are the same thing. This equivalence is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164040,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called the equivalence principle. We normally \nthink of the equivalence principle as being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the epiphany that led Einstein to his general \ntheory of relativity, but it’s older than that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175520,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A hundred years ago, Einstein realized that \nthere should be no experiment that could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179720,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distinguish between the feeling of weight you \nhave when supported in a gravitational field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the same feeling when being accelerated at \nthe correct rate in empty space. This statement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the equivalence principle requires that \ninertial mass—the mass resisting acceleration—is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193040,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same as gravitational mass—the mass \nthat responds to a gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But over 300 years prior to Einstein, this \nguy named Galileo demonstrated the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202600,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing when he dropped a pair of balls \noff the top of the Tower of Pisa. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had different weights but the same size \nso air resistance was equal. He found  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212080,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they reached the ground at the same \ntime. We’ve now done this experiment in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215960,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the near-vacuum of the Moon’s surface with a \nhammer and a feather, giving the same result."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220960,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Around a century after Galileo’s Pisa-drop, \nNewton came along and figured out the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225440,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equations that tell us why. Let’s say that \nthe gravitational mass in Newton’s gravity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "law is the same as the inertial mass \nin his second law of mechanics. Then,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235800,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when we calculate the acceleration experienced \nby a massive object in a gravitational field,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239920,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these two masses cancel out. In other words, more \nmassive objects feel a stronger pull but are also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "harder to accelerate. Because of this, hammers \nand feathers fall at the same rate on the Moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravity is always attractive because gravitational \ncharge—aka mass—is always positive. Compare to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257880,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetism, which can be attractive or \nrepulsive due to electric charge being positive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263120,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or negative. So what happens if we allow mass to \nbe positive OR negative. We covered the details  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our previous video, but long story short \nis that two positive masses behave as normal,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two negative masses repel each other, \nand a positive mass is repelled by a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279240,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative mass while the negative mass \nis attracted to the positive. That last  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284080,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part means a positive and negative mass would \nchase each other, accelerating at a constant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rate forever. That breaks conservation of \nmomentum and energy and so can’t be right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The thing that really breaks physics in this \nscenario is that we allowed negative inertial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299720,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass, which will always move in the wrong \ndirection to an applied force. But perhaps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306000,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can fix this by separating our mass types. \nWhat if inertial mass is always positive, but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310920,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational mass can be positive or negative. \nThen a pair of positive and negative gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "masses will mutually repel each other, which \nis just what we want for anti-gravity. But if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we say that inertial and gravitational mass \nare not equivalent then we’ve overturned the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326000,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equivalence principle. We should not do that \nlightly. After all, it’s one of the founding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331000,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "axioms of general relativity, which is itself an \ninsanely successful theory. But it turns out that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338280,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the type of repulsive gravity we’re looking \nfor we have to break the equivalence principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Speaking of general relativity, let’s see how \nnegative masses work here. In GR gravity is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350480,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explained as mass causing the fabric of space and \ntime to curve so that things move differently due  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to that curvature. In the classic rubber sheet \nanalogy, objects with positive mass depress the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sheet causing straight lines to deflect towards \nthem. Hypothetical negative gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365320,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "masses would be depicted by pinching the sheet \nupwards, causing straight lines to move away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370000,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This picture makes it somewhat intuitive why the \nmotion of particles seems to only depend on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374480,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shape of the gravitational field—which itself \ndepends only on the gravitational mass of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378240,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "central object. Objects follow geodesics—the \n“straightest lines” in curved space regardless  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of their own mass. In fact we can also think of \nthis as the gravitational and inertial masses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390760,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "canceling out, just like with Newtonian gravity. \nWe can see that canceling in something called the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396560,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geodesic equation, which is the equation of motion \nfor a particle in general relativity. Normally the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass of the moving object doesn’t appear in \nit, just like it doesn't in the Newtonian acceleration  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408800,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equation. But it’s actually hidden. If we separate \ninertial and gravitational mass we see it here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the geodesic equation also tells us how \npositive and negative gravitational masses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421200,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interact. We’re going to keep inertial mass \npositive because anything else is insane. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative gravitational masses change the sign \nof the gravitational field or the sign of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass of the interacting particle or both. If \nboth masses are p ositive or both negative,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the minus signs get canceled and we have \nregular gravitational attraction. But if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444680,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only one of those masses is negative \nthen we end up with a new minus sign  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448600,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between the field and the object. The \neffect is gravitational repulsion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454400,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, something with \na negative gravitational mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457680,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would fall upwards in Earth’s gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, great, so to make an antigravity engine \nwe just need a negative gravitational mass.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467680,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what’s all this about antimatter \npossibly having this property? An antimatter "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471860,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle has the same mass \nas its regular matter counterpart,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475400,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but is opposite in many quantum properties. \nThat doesn’t sound helpful—if antimatter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has the same mass as regular matter \nthen where’s our negative mass? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remember we just broke the equivalence \nprinciple—at least in our imagination—so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490640,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maybe antimatter can have positive inertial \nmass but negative gravitational mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495400,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two reasons we might think this is the \ncase: First: based on its response to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electromagnetic field we know antimatter \nhas positive inertial mass, but we’ve never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually measured its gravitational mass. Well, \nactually, just recently we finally did and I’ll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513680,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tell you the results in a bit. But we’ve had \na lot of years to come up with reason two:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519640,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are theoretical motivations for thinking \nthat maybe antimatter has negative gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524240,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass. To understand this, we need to understand \nthe theory behind antimatter a bit better."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529720,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Antimatter is what you get when you take \nregular matter and do three things to it:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flip its charge, reflect its spatial coordinates \n- so clockwise particles become counterclockwise,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and reverse its time axis—which is equivalent \nto reversing all of its motion vectors. These  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545240,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transformations are called charge \nconjugation, parity inversion and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time reversal, and applied together we have \na CPT transformation. CPT-transform regular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter and it becomes antimatter. And of course \nthere’s a homework episode for you on this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561040,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most physicists think that our universe \nhas to be CPT symmetric—which means that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565040,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although the laws of physics change if you \nperform any of these transformations separately,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569960,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you perform all three together the \nlaws of physics should be exactly the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same. CPT-transform the universe and you \nget an antimatter universe that follows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579680,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exactly the same laws of physics \nas our regular-matter universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582760,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s at least one big reason \nto wonder if CPT-symmetry is broken,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587680,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that’s because almost all of the \nmatter in the universe is regular matter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591080,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with hardly any antimatter. In a perfectly \nCPT-symmetric universe, matter and antimatter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably should have been created in equal \nquantities soon after the big bang. If we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found out that CPT symmetry is broken, and how it \nbreaks, we could perhaps solve this big mystery."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I digress. We’re here to build a flying \ncar. And understanding the CPT-symmetry can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "help us here too. If this symmetry is true then \nall the laws of physics should look the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under CPT transformation—so an antimatter \nuniverse should run by the same equations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "including the equations of general relativity. \nAnd indeed, if you apply a CPT transformation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630720,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the geodesic equation you get some minus \nsigns as you reverse the flow of time and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636040,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flip the dimensions of space, etc. But \nthey cancel out and give you exactly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639920,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same equation you started with. That’s \nequivalent to switching both of a pair of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mutually-gravitating objects to antimatter. \nThey’d still attract each other. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can’t even say if they both have positive \nor negative mass—the math looks the same as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658280,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long as they have the same sign of mass. General \nrelativity in general seems to be CPT invariant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666080,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what happens if you CPT-transform only \npart of the equation—if you turn one of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pair of masses into antimatter. The exact \nway a CPT transformation interacts with GR  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677960,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is still being debated, but at least some \nphysicists have argued that the result on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683520,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the geodesic equation is to introduce a minus \nsign—either to the field or to the mass in motion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690560,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depending on which you turn into antimatter. But \nmathematically, that’s equivalent to changing one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the gravitational masses negative. So, \nsome ways of thinking about the symmetry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of general relativity gives antimatter \nnegative gravitational mass. Remember  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706600,
    "dDurationMs": 6630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that this is a contested interpretation, but the result would be gravitational repulsion and hoverboards. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713230,
    "dDurationMs": 4716,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the very least it's worth finding out whether anti-matter falls up or down?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717946,
    "dDurationMs": 7094,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whether for the awesomeness of our vehicular future or just to see if there's any weirdness in the gravitational interaction of antimatter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that could poke holes in CPT-symmetry and \nhelp us answer some very big questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730880,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, let’s get to the experiment finally. What \nhappens when you drop an apple made of antimatter?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And to start with, why is it so hard to do this \nexperiment? It’s because we don’t have antimatter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744680,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apples. Antimatter is extremely hard to create \nand keep contained. It instantly annihilates when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it encounters regular matter. At best we have \nantimatter atoms. The difficulty is also due to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757360,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fact that the gravitational force is so weak \ncompared to other fundamental forces. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762440,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we wanted to measure the effects of gravity \non a free falling electron in the Earth's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766680,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field, it would be like trying \nto measure the force of gravity that a human  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771440,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "body exerts on a feather. Not that astronauts \nbody on the dropped feather—but the force that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your body here on Earth exerts on that feather \non the moon. Add the fact that any antiparticle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784240,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we analyze will be subject to vastly greater \nforces from ambient electromagnetic fields  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and this becomes an intensely difficult \nexperiment. But not an impossible one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795160,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this is a CERN experiment that has been years \nin the making. It starts with the antimatter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "production line by the ALPHA collaboration. This \nteam has managed to create and magnetically trap  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805920,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "100s of stable anti-hydrogen, consisting of a \npositron orbiting an anti-proton. Their sister  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812960,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collaboration, ALPHA-g, then takes these atoms and \ndrops them in freefall in a vacuum chamber. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819560,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sounds simple, but it's an extremely delicate \noperation. Because of the extreme smallness  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824640,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the gravitational force on these atoms and the \nother effects that jostle them around, they don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829720,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all just tinkle downwards. Some move up, some move \ndown, and at different rates. But by measuring the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rate and timing of anti-hydrogen atoms that reach \nboth the top and bottom of the chamber compared to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841120,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regular hydrogen, the relative gravitational \nacceleration on the atoms can be calculated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848640,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And after the first run of the \nALPHA-g experiment it turns out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852920,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that acceleration is … downwards. Yup, \nthey are almost certainly not falling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860440,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up. That likely dashes our hopes of \nrepulsive gravity from antimatter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However there is tentative evidence for \nsomething interesting. The gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872160,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force may be slightly weaker on these \nanti-hydrogen atoms compared to regular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hydrogen. This fairly preliminary data \nsuggests that the gravitational acceleration  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883720,
    "dDurationMs": 11200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the anti-hydrogen 0.75 plus or minus 0.13 times that \nof regular hydrogen atoms, meaning it might  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fall a bit slower. That actually sounds like a big  difference, but it’s less than a 3 sigma result,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so could be a fluke. In fact the scientists of the ALPHA-g paper do claim that the gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acceleration of antimatter is consistent \nwith it being the same as regular matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914360,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s because misleading sub-3-sigma results \nhappen all the time, and when it’s something  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really unexpected like this, it’s much more \nlikely to be a statistical fluke. Remember,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re dealing with only 100s of atoms here so \nrandom variation is going to be significant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930480,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, if there is a difference in the \ngravitational force felt by matter versus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934640,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antimatter it would be extremely exciting. \nIt would mean a violation of CPT symmetry.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940280,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would mean that the universe treats \nantimatter differently to regular matter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943800,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps explaining why there’s \nthis enormous imbalance between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947080,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quantities of each. ALPHA will \ncontinue to build anti-hydrogen atoms,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and ALPHA-g will continue to drop them—and \nthey will fall down. But whether they fall at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957920,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same rate as regular hydrogen will be known with \nincreasing confidence over the next few years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we may not have a clear \npath to our levitating cars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least yet. But we do have a path to either \nsolidifying or breaking CPT symmetry,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974040,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so understanding what’s currently the \nmost fundamental symmetry of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to 80,000 Hours, a project of Effective \nVentures, for supporting PBS. 80,000 Hours is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nonprofit that aims to help people have a positive \nimpact with their career. In an average career  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990800,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll work 40 hours a week, 50 weeks a year for \n40 years. That adds up to 80,000 hours and how you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spend that time is a very important decision for \nyou and the impact you can have on the world. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003920,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "80,000 Hours offers research-backed guides for \nhow to find careers that tackle pressing issues  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(including many in science and tech), a podcast \nwhere you have in-depth conversations with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experts on these issues, as well as a curated \njob board they keep up to date with jobs they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021240,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "think will make a difference. Everything they \nprovide is free and their only aim is to help  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027040,
    "dDurationMs": 8010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you find a fulfilling, high-impact career. \nTo learn more go to 80000hours.org/spacetime"
    } ]
  } ]
}
